[# scripts/v_fork_register.cxd — the V fork DIVERGENCE REGISTER.

   CX builds against a permanent fork of the V compiler (cx-home/v), not
   upstream vlang/v. This file is the register of that divergence: the
   upstream commit the patch series sits on, the reason each patch FAMILY
   exists, and one row per fork commit assigning it to a family.

   It is a CONTROL file, not documentation. `make check-v-fork`
   (scripts/check_v_fork_patches.cx) compares this register against the
   actual commit set in third_party/v and fails when the two disagree in
   EITHER direction:

     - a fork commit with no row here          -> UNDOCUMENTED divergence
     - a row here with no matching fork commit -> STALE register entry

   Deliberately absent: the commit SUBJECT. Subjects live in git and the
   gate prints them; duplicating them here would create a second copy to
   drift. What a maintainer writes down is the WHY, and the why lives on
   the family, not the commit.

   ## Maintenance posture (RULED: VC-1)

   The fork is permanent. It is not a staging area for patches on their way
   upstream, and the gate no longer treats an unmerged upstream patch as a
   pending state to be resolved. Two upstream issues were tracked that way
   until 2026-08-24 (vlang/v#27178, vlang/v#27179); both closed without
   merging, and the campaign audit then found that upstream 0.5.2 already
   carries both changes by another route, so neither is fork divergence at
   all. The tracked rows retired with the expectation.

   ## Adding a patch

   1. Land the commit on cx-patches-0.18 and move the submodule pin.
   2. Add a `[patch sha=<full-40-char-sha> family=<id>]` row below.
   3. If no family fits, add a `[family]` element with a `reason=` that
      says why CX carries it permanently and what would retire it. An
      empty reason fails the gate — the reason IS the documentation.

   ## Rebasing onto a newer upstream V

   Every SHA changes, so the whole register goes red at once. That is the
   intent: a rebase is the moment the entire inventory must be
   re-confirmed, not silently carried. Update `upstream-base` and
   regenerate the rows from
   `git -C third_party/v log --no-merges --reverse --format=%H <base>..HEAD`.
#]

[v-fork-register
  submodule-path='third_party/v'
  upstream-base=7647ce1c6fad63b5578bc07883139906de74b2f8
  upstream-base-label='vlang/v 0.5.2 — commit "V 0.5.2 changelog", 2026-07-13, an ancestor of vlang/v master'

  [family id=vgc
    reason='Architecture-E memory management: the Perceus reference-counting front line plus the precise stop-the-world vgc backstop, which is the DEFAULT collector for ordinary C-backend programs. CX depends on precise collection and on RSS tracking the recent working set; bundled Boehm delivers neither. Upstream V has no architecture E and no plan for one, so this family retires only if upstream adopts a precise collector.']

  [family id=gc-e-cgen
    reason='Codegen correctness under -gc e and Perceus: generated free functions, option and result element frees, eager-drop soundness for aliased call results, thread-return-box pinning across exit-to-join, and the markused interaction that keeps a map-free program from retaining map internals. Inseparable from the vgc family — the collector is only sound if cgen emits the frees it assumes. Retires with vgc.']

  [family id=usecache
    reason='Soundness of -usecache and build-module: compiler-identity cache salt, no cross-translation-unit tentative definitions, type-table validation of cached module layers before linking, deterministic embed_file resolution and embedded-asset invalidation. The failure mode of this class is SILENT MISCOMPILATION (cx-private #151, #520, #855, #864), and CX build times depend on the cache being usable. Retires when upstream V carries equivalent validation.']

  [family id=net
    reason='vlib/net capabilities the CX transports require: DTLS-over-UDP with HelloVerifyRequest cookies (the marine NMEA feed is UDP), min and max TLS protocol-version bounds, in-protocol close_notify, runtime server-identity certificate rotation, explicit isize conversion at the C.sendto boundary, and the SERVER-role TLS handshake over an ALREADY-ACCEPTED TcpConn (new_ssl_server_conn + SSLConn.accept_conn) that an explicit upgrade — SMTP STARTTLS RFC 3207, IMAP STARTTLS RFC 9051 §6.2.1 — needs and that upstream net.mbedtls does not expose: it offers the client role over an existing socket (SSLConn.connect) and the server role over a socket the listener itself bound (SSLListener.accept), and not the third combination. Upstream V has no DTLS wrapper and no explicit-upgrade entry point. Retires only if upstream grows them.']

  [family id=cgen
    reason='General codegen capability and performance that is independent of the GC: honoring thread_local on a __global, direct indexed stores for the in-place reuse map, and forwarding a propagating call result directly when the callee result type is the function own type. CX-agnostic and upstreamable in principle; carried because the upstream path did not take them.']

  [family id=libgc
    reason='Handling of the bundled Boehm libgc amalgamation: thread-local allocation flipped ON, and parallel mark capped to a single marker on macOS. Non-E builds and the macOS hardened runtime both depend on this; the vendored amalgamation is upstream V own copy, so the patch has to live in the fork.']

  [family id=build-toolchain
    reason='Build reproducibility and fork safety: pinned vc bootstrap, tccbin and macports-legacy-support fetches; C-error bug reports made opt-in because a fork must not phone home; -gc e on macOS resolving straight to the system cc because tcc has no Mach-O thread-local storage; and v up refusing to rebase this patched fork by default. These exist BECAUSE the fork is a fork — they cannot be upstreamed and are permanent by construction.']

  [family id=vlib
    reason='Assorted vlib correctness the fork carries: os execute_capture using the public spawn.h rather than private posix_spawn externs, sync panicking loudly when a pthread lock or unlock fails instead of guarding nothing, map value_ptr for a by-ref get with one probe and no value copy, and db.redis handling the RESP3 null reply. Each is CX-agnostic and upstreamable in principle.']

  [family id=compress
    reason='The dynamic-Huffman block emitter for compress.deflate. Upstream V emits fixed-Huffman blocks only, which costs roughly 35% of the achievable ratio on literal-dominated input (measured 2000 raw: fixed 1943, dynamic 1241) — and that emitter is what cx-stdlib/zip and bytes:gzip-compress ship to a customer. The fork adds a canonical-Huffman table builder and an RFC 1951 3.2.7 dynamic header over the EXISTING LZ77 tokenizer, so every entry point emits the smaller of fixed/dynamic from one tokenize pass and stays a pure function of its input (which is what cx-stdlib/zip determinism rests on). CX-agnostic and upstreamable in principle; retires the day upstream V carries a dynamic emitter.']

  [family id=checker
    reason='Type-checker completeness the fork carries because a gap there fails as a C error, not a V one. Today: a match-EXPRESSION arm is type-checked against a `.result` return type. `same_sumtype_variant` compared the `.option` flag and never `.result`, and `Type.idx()` masks both off, so a `!T` and a plain `T` were the same variant to the checker — an arm yielding `!T` passed where the sumtype was wanted and cgen then assigned a `_result_T` into it. The failure surfaces only in the generated C, naming generated identifiers and a line in a temporary .c file with no source location, which on a codebase mid-refactor reads as "the rewrite broke codegen" rather than "an ordinary type error the checker declined to report" (cx-private#1213, found during the #1119 representation campaign and worse at #1204, where many conversions land exactly in match-expression arms). CX-agnostic and upstreamable in principle; retires the day upstream V types those arms.']

  [family id=diag
    reason='Gated diagnostic probes and localizers, all off by default and behind -d flags: the sweep-while-live oracle, closure and holder watches, the vgc_verify rootfinder, the stack-base helper. They cost nothing when off and they are the instrumentation every vgc soundness investigation has started from (cx-private #57, #58, #63, #145). Permanent for as long as the collector is.']

  [family id=docs
    reason='Source comments made host-agnostic. The fork must not name the downstream sponsor or CX-specific context in its own source — the PB and CX company boundary. Permanent: it is a property of the fork being a separate public repository.']

  [family id=bench
    reason='bench/parallel-alloc drivers moved into per-program subdirectories so v test works over that directory again. Carried because the allocator benchmarks in that tree are the measurement harness for the vgc family.']

  [family id=ssh
    reason='The SSH client and its SFTP v3 subsystem behind cx-platform/sftp (cx-private#1457, RULED: SSHL-1): libssh2, vendored in thirdparty/libssh2 and built over the fork own thirdparty/mbedtls as its crypto backend, so CX carries one crypto stack, with vlib/net/ssh2 the only V code that names a libssh2 symbol. Upstream V has no SSH client and no plan for one; a fresh SSH implementation in V was the rejected option, a security-critical protocol whose every primitive would be ours to get right. Retires only if upstream V grows an SSH client over its own TLS stack.']

